\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Grid discretization of the simplex of spectra for $d_A=3$. (a) Different values of $F(\alpha )$ are denoted by different colors. The dots denote the grid $G$, separated by a certain distance $1/m$. Since entanglement measures are symmetric functions on the spectrum, only the points with $\alpha _1\geq \alpha _2\geq \alpha _3$ (dashed region, filled dots) need to be imposed. (b) Cut along a one-parameter family of spectra. Although $P^*\leq F$ holds at all grid points, it is possible that $P^*>F$ between any two points by a value $\epsilon $. This value decreases as we refine the grid.}}{11}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:grid}{{3}{11}{Grid discretization of the simplex of spectra for $d_A=3$. (a) Different values of $F(\alpha )$ are denoted by different colors. The dots denote the grid $G$, separated by a certain distance $1/m$. Since entanglement measures are symmetric functions on the spectrum, only the points with $\alpha _1\geq \alpha _2\geq \alpha _3$ (dashed region, filled dots) need to be imposed. (b) Cut along a one-parameter family of spectra. Although $P^*\leq F$ holds at all grid points, it is possible that $P^*>F$ between any two points by a value $\epsilon $. This value decreases as we refine the grid}{figure.3}{}}
